---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Tft from '../../components/Tft.astro';
import { caseStudies } from '../../lib/projects';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const project = caseStudies[0];

const sections = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'chain', label: 'The chain' },
  { id: 'hardware', label: 'Hardware' },
  { id: 'model', label: 'The model' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'Results' },
  { id: 'dashboard', label: 'The dashboard' },
  { id: 'honest', label: 'Honest status' },
  { id: 'build', label: 'Build it yourself' },
];
---
<CaseLayout project={project} sections={sections} prev={{ slug: 'swarm', title: 'Swarm robotics' }} next={{ slug: 'attendx', title: 'AttendX' }}>
  <!-- 01 overview -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="overview">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">01</span><span class="rail__label">Overview</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>

    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      CryNet is a monitor for rooms with more than one cot: a NICU bay, a maternity ward, a
      daycare, a twin nursery at home. Two microphone nodes sit beside the cots and listen
      continuously. When a node hears something interesting, it freezes two seconds of audio and
      sends them to a central hub over a short-range radio link. The hub runs a small neural
      network on the clip and decides whether it is an infant cry. If it is, the hub names the cot
      on its display and sends an alert over Bluetooth, and a wristband turns that into a
      vibration.
    </p>

    <div class="choose" data-reveal>
      <div class="note">
        <p class="note__title">Choice 1: who speaks</p>
        <p><strong>The nodes decide when to speak; the hub decides what it means.</strong>
        A node that hears nothing interesting sends almost nothing. That is what makes the radio
        work with two nodes sharing one channel, and it keeps a battery node alive for a whole
        shift.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        <p class="note__title">Choice 2: who thinks</p>
        <p><strong>Only the hub runs the model.</strong> One ESP32-S3 with 8 MB of PSRAM is a far
        better home for a neural network than two small battery-powered nodes. It also means the
        model gets retrained and re-flashed in one place instead of at every cot.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        <p class="note__title">Choice 3: who warns</p>
        <p><strong>The wearable is a dumb, reliable terminal.</strong> It runs no model. Receive
        alert, vibrate. Its only job is to be trusted with something the caregiver must not miss.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="media-grid media-grid--2" data-reveal>
      <Tft src={url('/img/crynet/tft-listening.png')} bezel={false} alt="Hub panel, listening: two cot cards online" caption="LISTENING · two cots linked, live level bars per cot" />
      <Tft src={url('/img/crynet/tft-alert.png')} bezel={false} alt="Hub panel with a cry detected on cot one, named, at 97 percent" caption="CRY DETECTED · cot named, confidence on the panel" />
    </div>
    <p class="figure-caption mono" data-reveal>panel renders from the running hub · ILI9341 · 320×240 · the same pixels the device draws</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 the chain -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="chain">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">02</span><span class="rail__label">The chain</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The path of a cry, with the constants the firmware actually uses:
    </p>

    <ol class="chain" data-reveal>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">capture</span><span>16 kHz, 16-bit I²S off the INMP441, continuously. The node removes the DC bias the microphone sits at (~+18000 counts).</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">track the floor</span><span>A running noise floor: <code>g_floor += (g_rms − g_floor) × 0.002</code>, slow enough to ride out a scream and fast enough to follow a room.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">trigger</span><span>Level = 3 × floor, clamped to [0.005, 0.030]. Crossing it freezes a two-second window <em>centred</em> on the crossing, so the onset is kept rather than clipped off.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">offer</span><span>The node streams the window as a 286-fragment ESP-NOW burst (per-fragment CRC-16, whole-window CRC-32) and holds it until the hub pulls it.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">pull</span><span>The hub arbitrates between cots, reassembles the audio, and refuses the window outright if any sample clipped (peak ≥ 31,000 of 32,767; that saturation once made it name the wrong cot for 42 seconds).</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">front end</span><span>Zero-padded 512-pt Hann FFT, 81 frames at hop 400 → 257 bins → 64 log-Mel bands → dB floored at −80 → per-window standardisation. Straight C on the ESP-DSP FFT: <strong>41.7 ms</strong>.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">decide</span><span>INT8 quantisation, TFLite Micro invoke with ESP-NN SIMD kernels: <strong>101.8 ms</strong> for a 122,770-parameter CNN.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">announce</span><span>Only a near-saturated window announces alone; otherwise two consecutive windows must agree, the second carrying real energy and the right crest. On real room audio the model never sits between P(cry) 0.215 and 0.992; the rule is built on that gap.</span></li>
      <li><span class="mono chain__k">reach the wrist</span><span>A six-byte alert packet over BLE → 2.5 s haptic pulse train and a screen that names <em>every</em> crying cot (two-at-once staged on the bench: mask held at 0x03 for fifteen seconds).</span></li>
    </ol>

    <div class="note note--amber" data-reveal>
      <p class="note__title">The finding that forced the design</p>
      <p>The original fixed trigger was RMS ≥ 0.030. The confusion session showed that a cry at one
      metre never reaches it: median RMS 0.0070, 0 of 590 records over threshold. So the system was
      blind at one metre <em>by trigger</em>, not by hearing. Asked anyway, the model still caught
      18 of 44 windows. That measurement is why the adaptive 3×-floor trigger exists. After the
      change: 39 trigger events per minute at one metre instead of 0, and the hub asks 5–11 times a
      minute instead of 1–2.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 hardware -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="hardware">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">03</span><span class="rail__label">Hardware</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>

    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Cot node × 2</dt>
      <dd>ESP32-C3 Pro Mini + INMP441 I²S MEMS mic. GPIO4/5/6 for I²S, node ids 1 and 2 set over serial and kept in NVS, verified to survive a reflash. ESP-NOW channel 6. 75 % flash, 50 % RAM.</dd>
      <dt>Hub</dt>
      <dd>ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 (dual-core LX7 @ 240 MHz, 512 KB SRAM, 8 MB PSRAM, 16 MB flash) + 2.8″ ILI9341 TFT on GPIO10–14, backlight GPIO4. 13.0 % RAM, 63.6 % flash.</dd>
      <dt>Wearable</dt>
      <dd>ESP32-C3 SuperMini + 0.96″ SSD1306 OLED + coin ERM motor on a BC547 low-side switch (GPIO7 → 2.2 kΩ → base, 22 Ω stall cap). 52 % flash, 5 % RAM.</dd>
    </dl>

    <div class="media-grid media-grid--2" data-reveal>
      <figure class="figure">
        <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/crynet/photo-colour-check.jpg')} alt="Photograph of the physical hub running its colour-check screen" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <figcaption>COLOUR CHECK on the real panel. The calibration screen the TFT runs before it is trusted to render alerts.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="figure">
        <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/crynet/panel-after.jpg')} alt="The TFT panel after the display fix" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <figcaption>Panel after the backlight/contrast fix; before/after shots are kept in the dossier as display evidence.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="media-grid media-grid--2" data-reveal>
      <figure class="figure">
        <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/crynet/units-load.jpg')} alt="The full CryNet system in printed enclosures: wearable, hub booting to its splash screen, both cot nodes" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <figcaption>IN THE SHELLS · the wearable on its strap, the hub rising to its splash, both cot nodes beside it</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="figure">
        <div class="figure__frame">
          <video src={url('/img/crynet/inside-hardware.mp4')} poster={url('/img/crynet/inside-hardware-poster.jpg')} controls muted playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
        </div>
        <figcaption>INSIDE THE SHELLS · the wearable going together on camera: pack, buck and board into the enclosure, lid screwed down</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="media-grid media-grid--2" data-reveal>
      <figure class="figure">
        <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/crynet/units-cry.jpg')} alt="The real hub panel showing CRY DETECTED on cot one at 100 percent while the wearable names cot one" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <figcaption>THE ALERT, END TO END · the hub names cot 1 at 100 %, the wrist names it too: event 63, 8 s ago</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="figure">
        <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/crynet/assembling.jpg')} alt="Me assembling the CryNet wearable enclosure at the NASCOMSOFT bench" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <figcaption>AT THE NASCOMSOFT BENCH · the wearable enclosure coming together by hand</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="media-grid media-grid--2" data-reveal>
      <figure class="figure">
        <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/crynet/breadboarding.jpg')} alt="CryNet nodes and the TFT panel on breadboards during bring-up, firmware open on a laptop" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <figcaption>BEFORE THE SHELLS · nodes and the TFT on breadboards, the firmware open on the laptop</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="figure">
        <div class="figure__frame"><img src={url('/img/crynet/wearable.jpg')} alt="The wearable prototype on a breadboard, OLED showing CRYNET LINK and a cleared screen" loading="lazy" /></div>
        <figcaption>THE WEARABLE, ON THE BENCH · OLED showing LINK and a cleared screen, tape label and all</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 the model -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="model">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">04</span><span class="rail__label">The model</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>

    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      Before training anything I profiled every public infant-cry corpus on Kaggle and Hugging Face:
      sample rates, durations, F0, spectral flatness, real versus synthetic. Three of the five
      mirrors of one popular dataset were rejected outright. Their "silence" class is a constant
      1,225 Hz tone at RMS 0.22, and their "noise" class is renamed ESC-50 files. Training on that
      teaches dataset artifacts, not cries.
    </p>

    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Primary: ICD</dt><dd>28,478 clips, all 16 kHz mono / 5 s, five balanced folds. Cries from three independent corpora (hundreds of different infants); negatives are VoxCeleb speech, domestic noise, human non-speech, cat meows and silence.</dd>
      <dt>Cry diversity</dt><dd>Baby Cry 4-class: 4,871 real cries, LUFS-normalised, VAD-trimmed, deduplicated. Held out as an unseen cross-corpus test.</dd>
      <dt>Hard negatives</dt><dd>ESC-50 (1,960 non-cry clips: speech, laughing, vacuum, alarms, knocks) and a 1,000-clip snoring corpus: the confusion class a nursery monitor hits constantly.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The network is a compact CNN over the 81×64 log-Mel spectrogram: 122,770 parameters, 135 KB
      as INT8. Two decisions mattered more than the architecture:
    </p>

    <ul class="body bullets" data-reveal>
      <li><strong>The front end must match training exactly.</strong> Log-Mel computed by librosa and by naive firmware code differ enough to destroy accuracy, so the training notebook generates the Mel filterbank and Hann window as C tables, and the compiled front end is parity-tested against Python, with a measured worst-case deviation of <strong>7 × 10⁻⁵</strong>, and on-chip ≤ 1 LSB with ≤ 1 % of the 5,184 features differing.</li>
      <li><strong>Confusers get mined, not tolerated.</strong> Coughing produced a 57 % false-alarm rate; acoustically, a cough is a vocal expulsion. After the main training, the notebook scores its own training set, takes the 600 most cry-like non-cry windows and the 600 least cry-like cry windows, and fine-tunes at 3× lower learning rate. Only training rows are mined, so the held-out folds stay honest. Cross-corpus false alarms fell 0.109 → 0.004 on the validation run, and music false alarms 8.19 % → 0.21 % after the retrain.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="stats" data-reveal>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">0.9994</p><p class="stat__label">ICD validation AUC · fold 4</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">0.9993</p><p class="stat__label">ICD test AUC · fold 5</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">0.9984</p><p class="stat__label">Cross-corpus AUC on unseen corpora, unseen babies</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">−0.0014</p><p class="stat__label">INT8 vs float32 ΔAUC · 99.956 % decision agreement</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 results -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="results">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">05</span><span class="rail__label">Results</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      Measured on the desk and in the room, not only in the notebook. One caveat: every positive
      cry in these tests is a recording played through a phone speaker. The honest-status section
      below explains what that means.
    </p>

    <h3 class="h3" data-reveal>The confusion session: nine one-minute takes on the real microphone</h3>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      Speech, music, film and quiet, at desk / 30 cm / 1 m distances, cut into 308 non-cry windows:
    </p>

    <div class="stats" data-reveal>
      <div class="stat stat--green"><p class="stat__value tnum">0 / 308</p><p class="stat__label">False alarms on non-cry audio: speech, music, film, quiet</p></div>
      <div class="stat"><p class="stat__value tnum">86.4 %</p><p class="stat__label">Recall on the cry at 15 cm (38/44 windows)</p></div>
      <div class="stat stat--amber"><p class="stat__value tnum">0 / 44</p><p class="stat__label">Loudest music this room can produce, refused post-retrain</p></div>
      <div class="stat stat--plain"><p class="stat__value tnum">20.5 min</p><p class="stat__label">Ambient room with people talking, 0 false alarms</p></div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="h3" data-reveal>The live chain: hub + node + a real BLE client</h3>
    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Radio integrity</dt><dd><strong>286/286 fragments with CRC-32 MATCH</strong>, with a BLE client connected. 0 bad frames, 0 bad payloads over thousands of frames.</dd>
      <dt>Burst duration</dt><dd>2.06–5.19 s, median 3.55 s, down from 5.54–6.93 s before the airtime fixes.</dd>
      <dt>Request → verdict</dt><dd>2.23–5.35 s, mean 4.03 s, end to end.</dd>
      <dt>Demo session</dt><dd><strong>45 bursts, 45/45 complete</strong>: no stall, no timeout, no CRC error, no resend round. 26 alerts at P(cry) 0.9922–0.9961, 16 refusals at 0.0078–0.2148.</dd>
      <dt>Verdict gap</dt><dd>Nothing between 0.2148 and 0.9922 in the whole session. On real audio in this room the model is never ambivalent: it is either near-zero or saturated.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3 class="h3" data-reveal>The two-cot bench session, 2026-11-05/06</h3>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The session that says the <em>system</em> works, not just the components. Board identities
      were read from each boot banner before anything was flashed, and both cot ids survived the
      flash out of NVS.
    </p>
    <dl class="kv" data-reveal>
      <dt>Frames</dt><dd><strong>993 frames off the air, 993 parsed, 0 bad, 0 bad payload</strong>: both cots heard and online.</dd>
      <dt>A full burst</dt><dd>Node 2: 286/286 fragments in 0.99 s; <code>window crc32 0xA8FCEBD5 → MATCH</code>; the model ran on it and correctly refused room noise.</dd>
      <dt>Alert + cancel</dt><dd>Console <code>alert 2</code> → wrist <code>ALERT cot 2 type 1 conf 100 %</code>; <code>clear</code> → matching type-2 cancel on the wrist.</dd>
      <dt>The bug it caught</dt><dd>The wrist still read <code>0 cot(s)</code>: the BLE state packet published only when a cry started or stopped, so a cot <em>joining</em> never reached the caregiver. Fix: rebuild the eight-byte packet every cycle and publish whenever the cry mask, deaf mask or cot count moves. Verified on hardware: the wrist now reads <strong>2 cot(s)</strong>.</dd>
      <dt>Two at once</dt><dd>Two cots crying together staged 2026-11-20: the crying mask held at 0x03 for fifteen seconds, and the wrist screen names every crying cot (the alert packet names the loudest; one cry, one buzz).</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 dashboard -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="dashboard">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">06</span><span class="rail__label">The dashboard</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>

    <div class="dash" data-reveal>
      <div class="dash__copy">
        <p class="body">
          The monitor works with no network at all, but every cry the hub hears is written into
          its own flash first, and when it finds Wi-Fi it uploads what it has. The dashboard turns
          that record into something a caregiver can read: which cot, when, how long, how loud,
          and whether the nights are getting better or worse.
        </p>
        <ul class="body bullets">
          <li><strong>No dependencies.</strong> The server is one Python file using only the standard library and a SQLite file. <code>python dashboard/server.py</code> and it runs. A Cloudflare Worker build hosts a public copy: <a href="https://amazings-crynet.amazingadelowo.workers.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">amazings-crynet.amazingadelowo.workers.dev</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>A contract, tested.</strong> A hardware-free test builds the hub's upload body byte for byte, posts it, and checks what comes back, so the hub and the dashboard cannot drift apart silently.</li>
          <li><strong>The monitor wins over the record.</strong> The hub has one radio: if the router is not on channel 6, where ESP-NOW lives, the hub drops Wi-Fi and takes the radio back. A dashboard that looks healthy while the wrist has gone silent is the dangerous failure, so the firmware refuses to allow it.</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn" style="margin-top:1.5rem" href="https://amazings-crynet.amazingadelowo.workers.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the live dashboard ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal>
        <div class="figure__frame dash-shot">
          <img class="dash-shot__img dash-shot__img--dark" src={url('/img/crynet/dash-dark.png')} alt="The CryNet dashboard in the dark theme, demo data: episode totals, cries-by-day and night-by-night charts, the by-cot table" loading="lazy" />
          <img class="dash-shot__img dash-shot__img--light" src={url('/img/crynet/dash-light.png')} alt="The CryNet dashboard in the light theme, demo data" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <p class="figure-caption mono">the caregiver dashboard · shown with demo data</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 honest status -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="honest">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">07</span><span class="rail__label">Honest status</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      The dossier opens with this section before any results, because these facts shape how every
      number should be read. A project page that hides them is not worth much.
    </p>

    <div class="notes-grid" data-reveal>
      <div class="note note--rose">
        <p class="note__title">O-2: no real infant yet</p>
        <p>Every cry the model has been tested against is a recording played through a phone speaker:
        compressed, band-limited, EQ'd by a small loudspeaker in a plastic case. The negatives are
        authentic: this room, this microphone. On the proxy the model is strong; on a real infant
        it is unproven. This is the single largest accuracy risk in the project.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note note--amber">
        <p class="note__title">O-1d: no battery runtime measured</p>
        <p>The wearable has run from its Li-Po pouch and kept working, so the battery path is not
        wholly untried. But no unit's battery life has been measured; the runtime figures that
        exist are projections, labelled as such. During the pouch session the haptic buzz went
        missing; a sagging 3.3 V rail is the live candidate and the investigation is open.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        <p class="note__title">O-1c: what the zero watchdog does not cover yet</p>
        <p>The node's I²S zero watchdog is on the boards. The dossier records two limits: it
        detects exact zeros only, so a microphone stuck at a DC value is not caught, and the
        fault-injection test proved the code executes and refills a window, not that it recovers
        the original physical fault. The closing test is a played-cry end-to-end run behind a
        real recovery. The microphone also has a measured close-range blind spot (O-1g): a source
        loud enough to drive it to the rail produces a window the hub refuses outright.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        <p class="note__title">What the system is not</p>
        <p>It is not a certified infant monitor; it is an academic prototype. It is not a cause
        classifier; the model is binary, cry or no cry, on purpose (the BLE packet has one bit for
        it). And it is not a replacement for a parent. It is a second pair of ears that wakes
        someone up. There is no battery monitoring either, by decision.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 08 build it yourself -->
  <section class="cs-section" id="build">
    <div class="rail" data-reveal><span class="rail__num">08</span><span class="rail__label">Build it yourself</span><span class="rail__line"></span></div>
    <p class="body" data-reveal>
      Everything needed to reproduce the build is written down, because the project was documented
      as if the next reader is a stranger with a soldering iron.
    </p>

    <div class="dl-grid" data-reveal>
      <a class="dl" href="https://amazings-crynet.amazingadelowo.workers.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="dl__kind mono">Live · Cloudflare Worker</span>
        <span class="dl__title">The dashboard, hosted</span>
        <span class="dl__note">The public copy of the cry record: which cot, when, how long, how loud. Read-only, no login.</span>
        <span class="dl__meta mono">amazings-crynet.amazingadelowo.workers.dev ↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="dl" href={url('/docs/crynet-dossier.pdf')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="dl__kind mono">PDF · 21 chapters</span>
        <span class="dl__title">The two-node dossier</span>
        <span class="dl__note">Every design decision, measurement and dead end, in order, including the ones that changed the design.</span>
        <span class="dl__meta mono">17.5 MB ↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="dl" href={url('/docs/crynet-wiring.pdf')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="dl__kind mono">PDF · 5 sheets</span>
        <span class="dl__title">Wiring diagrams</span>
        <span class="dl__note">Node, hub, wearable, the system power chain and the charger pad map, generated with a layout checker that fails the build on label collisions.</span>
        <span class="dl__meta mono">200 KB ↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="dl" href="https://github.com/Amazo123/CryNet-TwoNode" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="dl__kind mono">Firmware · prebuilt</span>
        <span class="dl__title">Flash by double-click</span>
        <span class="dl__note">The repo ships built flash images for all three roles plus a <code>flash.bat</code>: press 1, 2 or 3, type the port, it backs up first and then writes. No compiler, no network.</span>
        <span class="dl__meta mono">GitHub · Amazo123 ↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="dl" href="https://github.com/Amazo123/CryNet-TwoNode" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <span class="dl__kind mono">Training · Kaggle-ready</span>
        <span class="dl__title">The notebook</span>
        <span class="dl__note">Downloads the corpora, builds the features, trains, mines hard examples, calibrates the threshold, quantises to INT8 and exports the firmware artifacts, validated end to end across 24 cells.</span>
        <span class="dl__meta mono">GitHub · Amazo123 ↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .choose { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0.9rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .media-grid { display: grid; gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .media-grid--2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }

  .cs-section > .figure { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .figure--wide { margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .figure :global(figcaption) { max-width: 70ch; }
  .figure :global(figcaption a) { color: var(--accent); }

  .figure-caption { margin-top: 0.6rem; color: var(--dim); font-size: 0.62rem; }

  /* chain */
  .chain { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .chain li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 150px minmax(0, 1fr);
